WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.357 --> 00:00:04.008
- You need to believe in
yourself, despite what everybody

00:00:04.008 --> 00:00:05.542
you love and trust tells you.

00:00:05.542 --> 00:00:07.858
Ben and I had a joint back
account, and we'd go to,

00:00:07.858 --> 00:00:11.045
if you made money acting,
it went into the account,

00:00:11.045 --> 00:00:13.936
and the account was only to be used if,

00:00:13.936 --> 00:00:15.985
if you were going to New
York for an audition.

00:00:15.985 --> 00:00:18.493
But I mean, we really
wrote "Good Will Hunting,"

00:00:18.493 --> 00:00:20.602
out of necessity, I mean,
we just needed a job.

00:00:20.602 --> 00:00:23.805
You don't get anywhere being,

00:00:23.805 --> 00:00:25.867
you know, genuflecting,
to, you know, somebody.

00:00:25.867 --> 00:00:29.454
- He's an American actor, film
producer, and screenwriter.

00:00:29.454 --> 00:00:31.495
He's ranked by Forbes
magazine, as one of the

00:00:31.495 --> 00:00:33.848
most bankable Hollywood
stars, and is among

00:00:33.848 --> 00:00:36.657
the highest grossing actors of all time.

00:00:36.657 --> 00:00:38.365
He's received numerous
accolades, including

00:00:38.365 --> 00:00:41.080
one Academy Award, from four nominations.

00:00:41.080 --> 00:00:42.920
He's Matt Damon, and here's my take on his

00:00:42.920 --> 00:00:44.714
Top 10 Rules for Success.

00:00:44.714 --> 00:00:46.385
Rule number one is our personal
favorite, and make sure

00:00:46.385 --> 00:00:47.870
to stick around all the way to the end

00:00:47.870 --> 00:00:50.449
for some special bonus clips.

00:00:50.449 --> 00:00:52.935
(bullseye sound effect)

00:00:52.935 --> 00:00:55.867
- When I was younger everybody
told me not to be an actor.

00:00:55.867 --> 00:00:58.290
To this day, I say that to
people, who come up to me,

00:00:58.290 --> 00:00:59.416
and say, I'm thinking of going

00:00:59.416 --> 00:01:00.968
into acting, what do you think?

00:01:00.968 --> 00:01:03.941
I say, absolutely not, it's
a terrible idea, don't do it.

00:01:03.941 --> 00:01:06.263
Because, that's what everyone said to me.

00:01:06.263 --> 00:01:09.513
And, I think if you're going
to make it in this business,

00:01:09.513 --> 00:01:13.575
that is so full of rejection and hardship,

00:01:13.575 --> 00:01:16.946
you need to believe in
yourself, despite what everybody

00:01:16.946 --> 00:01:18.351
you love and trust tells you.

00:01:18.351 --> 00:01:21.591
And, it's a very personal journey.

00:01:21.591 --> 00:01:25.469
And so, so that was
really helpful, I think,

00:01:25.469 --> 00:01:27.724
you know, the advice to
stay away from this career

00:01:27.724 --> 00:01:30.216
was really helpful in my
own understanding, that,

00:01:30.216 --> 00:01:32.062
it was that important that I pursue it.

00:01:32.062 --> 00:01:35.592
You know, and the first
job I got was a real job,

00:01:35.592 --> 00:01:36.797
besides commercials and things like that.

00:01:36.797 --> 00:01:39.810
Local radio commercials and things

00:01:39.810 --> 00:01:41.220
that kind of gave us the money.

00:01:41.220 --> 00:01:43.541
Ben and I had a joint bank
account, and we'd go to,

00:01:43.541 --> 00:01:46.722
and if you made money acting,
it went into the account,

00:01:46.722 --> 00:01:48.648
and the account was only to be used

00:01:48.648 --> 00:01:51.724
if you were going to New
York for an audition,

00:01:51.724 --> 00:01:54.460
or if we took $10 out to
like, play video games.

00:01:54.460 --> 00:01:55.951
(laughs)

00:01:55.951 --> 00:01:58.659
And, I got a line in the
movie "Mystic Pizza."

00:01:58.659 --> 00:02:00.682
And the line was, "Mom, do
you want my green stuff?"

00:02:00.682 --> 00:02:02.179
And literally, but it was a scene that was

00:02:02.179 --> 00:02:04.481
a big dinner table scene, Julia Roberts...

00:02:04.481 --> 00:02:05.314
- Yeah.

00:02:05.314 --> 00:02:07.529
- It was like, a big movie for her, and,

00:02:07.529 --> 00:02:09.929
it was this whole dinner table scene, and,

00:02:09.929 --> 00:02:12.065
so it took us three nights to shoot,

00:02:12.065 --> 00:02:14.577
down in Mystic, Connecticut,
and I was a minor,

00:02:14.577 --> 00:02:17.218
so my mother had to come
with me, and, you know,

00:02:17.218 --> 00:02:20.301
we shot from 5 to 5 every night, and,

00:02:21.920 --> 00:02:23.753
I just remember being,

00:02:24.859 --> 00:02:28.713
so in love with the process
of film, I just, I was like,

00:02:28.713 --> 00:02:31.579
this is, the greatest,
it was the best time

00:02:31.579 --> 00:02:33.764
I'd ever had in my life,
and it was, you know,

00:02:33.764 --> 00:02:35.795
I was like, this is amazing, I want this

00:02:35.795 --> 00:02:37.686
to never ever ever stop.

00:02:37.686 --> 00:02:38.938
Like, I loved it.

00:02:38.938 --> 00:02:41.591
- But I mean, we really
wrote "Good Will Hunting,"

00:02:41.591 --> 00:02:43.643
out of necessity, I mean,
we just needed a job.

00:02:43.643 --> 00:02:46.480
And, you know, as you all know,

00:02:46.480 --> 00:02:48.402
they don't really give those out here.

00:02:48.402 --> 00:02:50.083
(laughs)

00:02:50.083 --> 00:02:52.936
So, it was just, you know,

00:02:52.936 --> 00:02:56.415
two guys who were eager and
young, and had a lot of energy,

00:02:56.415 --> 00:02:58.153
and a lot of creative energy, and,

00:02:58.153 --> 00:03:00.788
that's a big, it was
always a big problem with,

00:03:00.788 --> 00:03:02.831
L.A. and New York, was you'd,

00:03:02.831 --> 00:03:05.072
you know, you'd meet these
other actors, and they'd,

00:03:05.072 --> 00:03:05.905
and it's like,

00:03:05.905 --> 00:03:07.433
you're raring to go, and there's nowhere

00:03:07.433 --> 00:03:09.601
to put that energy, and so, you know,

00:03:09.601 --> 00:03:11.960
it can get put in horrible
places, it can get put in,

00:03:11.960 --> 00:03:14.455
you know, drugs and all that, you know.

00:03:14.455 --> 00:03:16.230
Or you can try to somehow channel it

00:03:16.230 --> 00:03:19.053
into something that feels productive.

00:03:19.053 --> 00:03:20.908
And even if it doesn't,
nothing comes of it,

00:03:20.908 --> 00:03:22.826
it's still productive, in the
sense that you're working.

00:03:22.826 --> 00:03:24.082
Even if you're getting together in groups

00:03:24.082 --> 00:03:26.275
and doing scenes, or taking classes, or,

00:03:26.275 --> 00:03:28.390
trying to cobble together a screenplay,

00:03:28.390 --> 00:03:30.099
the energy's going somewhere.

00:03:30.099 --> 00:03:32.872
- You have never seemed,
shackled, Matt, over your career

00:03:32.872 --> 00:03:35.829
by the fear of needing
to maintain some sort

00:03:35.829 --> 00:03:37.540
of particular image,
you've always been happy

00:03:37.540 --> 00:03:40.124
to speak out on issues that
you are concerned about,

00:03:40.124 --> 00:03:43.559
or to do whatever
projects take your fancy.

00:03:43.559 --> 00:03:47.150
How have you managed to slip those chains?

00:03:47.150 --> 00:03:49.382
- I just never thought
that you could micro-manage

00:03:49.382 --> 00:03:51.175
an image, you know, I
just didn't, you know.

00:03:51.175 --> 00:03:55.174
And the things I've
spoken about, I've just,

00:03:55.174 --> 00:03:57.061
you know, are things that I believe, and,

00:03:57.061 --> 00:03:59.008
it doesn't mean that I'm
right, it just, you know,

00:03:59.008 --> 00:04:02.437
and I'm happy to learn and
evolve in my thinking, and,

00:04:02.437 --> 00:04:05.884
but, if I'm saying something,
it's because it's what

00:04:05.884 --> 00:04:08.788
I'm genuinely feeling, and,

00:04:08.788 --> 00:04:10.538
you know, and that's,

00:04:11.516 --> 00:04:13.619
I don't think I've taken
too many hits for that.

00:04:13.619 --> 00:04:15.834
My high school acting
teacher, Gerry Speca,

00:04:15.834 --> 00:04:18.142
who had a huge influence on me

00:04:18.142 --> 00:04:19.642
and Ben and Casey,

00:04:20.586 --> 00:04:23.250
five words, he said,
just do your work, kid.

00:04:23.250 --> 00:04:25.340
And he repeated that so many times to me,

00:04:25.340 --> 00:04:28.129
just do your work, kid,
and that is kind of,

00:04:28.129 --> 00:04:30.064
what I can always retreat to, you know,

00:04:30.064 --> 00:04:32.302
that's my touchstone if,

00:04:32.302 --> 00:04:35.623
you know, when in doubt,
just focus on my work,

00:04:35.623 --> 00:04:37.613
you know, if everything on
the movie's going wrong,

00:04:37.613 --> 00:04:41.366
all right, you know, I'll make
sure that my work is tight,

00:04:41.366 --> 00:04:44.451
and, go from there and it's
a great place to base from,

00:04:44.451 --> 00:04:45.754
and it's a good foundation.

00:04:45.754 --> 00:04:48.088
I looked at the odds, you know,

00:04:48.088 --> 00:04:51.967
like how am I even going to
break into this business?

00:04:51.967 --> 00:04:54.461
Right, all the good parts, you know,

00:04:54.461 --> 00:04:57.878
as an unknown actor, to get a great role,

00:04:58.966 --> 00:05:02.925
you know, every other
actor has to pass on it.

00:05:02.925 --> 00:05:03.758
Right.

00:05:03.758 --> 00:05:05.467
Every other actor has to pass on it.

00:05:05.467 --> 00:05:09.439
And then it gets opened
up to you, and thousands

00:05:09.439 --> 00:05:11.601
of other people like you, right.

00:05:11.601 --> 00:05:12.434
(audience laughs)

00:05:12.434 --> 00:05:13.435
And you all go.

00:05:13.435 --> 00:05:17.236
And, you know, there are a
few parts, like I was looking

00:05:17.236 --> 00:05:20.569
at the, you know, a big part when I was,

00:05:22.131 --> 00:05:24.818
younger, was "Primal
Fear," remember that movie,

00:05:24.818 --> 00:05:29.042
with Richard Gere, and
Edward Norton got the part.

00:05:29.042 --> 00:05:29.959
- [Interviewer] After everyone passed.

00:05:29.959 --> 00:05:32.344
- Yeah, I mean, so that part became open,

00:05:32.344 --> 00:05:33.862
every young actor knew it.

00:05:33.862 --> 00:05:36.829
I mean, we, I paid, with
money I did not have,

00:05:36.829 --> 00:05:40.150
to take dialect lessons, you know,

00:05:40.150 --> 00:05:42.118
hired a dialect coach,
cause the kid was, you know,

00:05:42.118 --> 00:05:44.622
his alter ego was from
Kentucky, and he was

00:05:44.622 --> 00:05:46.728
from somewhere else, and I
had never been to Kentucky,

00:05:46.728 --> 00:05:48.565
and so, you know,

00:05:48.565 --> 00:05:50.459
we knew that that was it.

00:05:50.459 --> 00:05:54.140
You know that was going
to launch somebody.

00:05:54.140 --> 00:05:57.403
There just weren't
opportunities, you know.

00:05:57.403 --> 00:06:00.268
And so, "Courage Under
Fire," was one of those roles

00:06:00.268 --> 00:06:02.822
that was small, it was really only,

00:06:02.822 --> 00:06:06.234
it was two scenes, right, I
had two scenes with Denzel.

00:06:06.234 --> 00:06:09.333
And then the rest of it
was, I'm the medic, and I'm,

00:06:09.333 --> 00:06:10.591
you know,

00:06:10.591 --> 00:06:13.111
you know, Tim Guinee, an actor who I love,

00:06:13.111 --> 00:06:15.126
who's in "Promised Land," actually,

00:06:15.126 --> 00:06:19.148
is like, bleeding out on my
lap for, so for six weeks,

00:06:19.148 --> 00:06:21.411
we're in El Paso, and he's
like, moaning, and I'm just

00:06:21.411 --> 00:06:22.342
sitting there, and I'm like,

00:06:22.342 --> 00:06:23.750
I don't think the camera's on us, man.

00:06:23.750 --> 00:06:26.583
(audience laughs)

00:06:29.018 --> 00:06:32.600
But, you know, so it was
a really thankless job,

00:06:32.600 --> 00:06:33.591
for both of us.

00:06:33.591 --> 00:06:34.782
(audience laughs)

00:06:34.782 --> 00:06:37.769
Except for, those two scenes
that I had with Denzel.

00:06:37.769 --> 00:06:40.228
So I had two days of work on
that movie, and so I went,

00:06:40.228 --> 00:06:41.972
well what do I do, you know, it's,

00:06:41.972 --> 00:06:46.941
it's told in flashback, so
let me see, how can I change,

00:06:46.941 --> 00:06:48.699
myself, what can I do?

00:06:48.699 --> 00:06:51.152
The guy's supposed to become
a heroin addict, okay.

00:06:51.152 --> 00:06:53.818
Well, so what do I do to make that

00:06:53.818 --> 00:06:55.987
seem like it happened,
and the only thing I could

00:06:55.987 --> 00:06:59.557
come up with, was, to lose
as much weight as I could.

00:06:59.557 --> 00:07:01.458
And then put it back on as fast as I could

00:07:01.458 --> 00:07:03.078
to go do the flashback stuff.

00:07:03.078 --> 00:07:04.386
And so that's what I did.

00:07:04.386 --> 00:07:06.039
- I heard that wasn't
good for you health, like,

00:07:06.039 --> 00:07:07.172
your doctor was surprised.

00:07:07.172 --> 00:07:08.198
- No it was terrible.

00:07:08.198 --> 00:07:11.386
It you know, it was bad
because I didn't, you know,

00:07:11.386 --> 00:07:14.688
I asked them for a nutritionist,

00:07:14.688 --> 00:07:17.521
which was probably $1500 or $2500.

00:07:18.729 --> 00:07:20.333
And they said no, and you know,

00:07:20.333 --> 00:07:22.686
I don't think they,

00:07:22.686 --> 00:07:24.038
in their defense, I don't
think they really knew

00:07:24.038 --> 00:07:26.473
what I was going to do.

00:07:26.473 --> 00:07:28.425
You know, and they probably
had requests like this

00:07:28.425 --> 00:07:30.386
all the time, and they think
they're vanity requests.

00:07:30.386 --> 00:07:32.953
But it was a serious request, and,

00:07:32.953 --> 00:07:34.749
and they turned it down, and so,

00:07:34.749 --> 00:07:37.874
I went to, we had a friend, we knew a guy.

00:07:37.874 --> 00:07:40.205
(audience laughs)

00:07:40.205 --> 00:07:43.192
Who was also a, he was Austrian.

00:07:43.192 --> 00:07:44.237
I mean, funnily enough,

00:07:44.237 --> 00:07:47.394
he was Austrian, and he
was trying to be an actor.

00:07:47.394 --> 00:07:49.168
But he literally, because he was Austrian,

00:07:49.168 --> 00:07:51.413
he had Arnold's accent.

00:07:51.413 --> 00:07:53.545
And he was a former body builder.

00:07:53.545 --> 00:07:57.466
So, he was really aiming at
a small bullseye, you know.

00:07:57.466 --> 00:07:58.410
(audience laughs)

00:07:58.410 --> 00:08:00.664
Cause his spot was
really taken by somebody.

00:08:00.664 --> 00:08:02.546
(audience laughs)

00:08:02.546 --> 00:08:04.546
But he, he put me on his

00:08:05.976 --> 00:08:08.809
program that he would do
for body building shows.

00:08:08.809 --> 00:08:11.314
But they would do that
program three weeks out.

00:08:11.314 --> 00:08:13.300
You know, they would do this really,

00:08:13.300 --> 00:08:14.945
kind of reckless thing, when they're,

00:08:14.945 --> 00:08:18.834
you know, and so those guys
walk onstage, and, like,

00:08:18.834 --> 00:08:20.720
you could push, they look
strong, but you could

00:08:20.720 --> 00:08:23.899
knock 'em over, they're really,
suffering at that point,

00:08:23.899 --> 00:08:26.548
to kind of leech every
bit of everything out,

00:08:26.548 --> 00:08:29.161
so that the veins and the muscles pop out.

00:08:29.161 --> 00:08:32.921
So, I did it for like, three
and a half or four months.

00:08:32.921 --> 00:08:34.983
Which was just, too
much, you know, too much.

00:08:34.983 --> 00:08:37.483
And it, but, at the same time,

00:08:38.525 --> 00:08:40.192
you know, it worked.

00:08:41.458 --> 00:08:43.831
It worked, and you know,
when I got "The Rainmaker,"

00:08:43.831 --> 00:08:46.261
a year or so later, Coppola said

00:08:46.261 --> 00:08:47.949
that he'd seen "Courage Under Fire."

00:08:47.949 --> 00:08:50.661
And that that made a big difference.

00:08:50.661 --> 00:08:53.452
And funnily enough, it was
Edward Norton and I, who were

00:08:53.452 --> 00:08:55.097
going against each other again for that.

00:08:55.097 --> 00:08:59.174
And "Courage Under Fire"
pushed me over the edge.

00:08:59.174 --> 00:09:01.757
And when I met Spielberg later,

00:09:02.749 --> 00:09:04.908
and he looked at me, and he said,

00:09:04.908 --> 00:09:06.242
what, you know,

00:09:06.242 --> 00:09:07.977
and I'd put myself on tape
for "Saving Private Ryan."

00:09:07.977 --> 00:09:09.010
I never heard anything,

00:09:09.010 --> 00:09:11.182
I don't think the tape ever got to him.

00:09:11.182 --> 00:09:13.052
The casting director maybe
looked at it and chucked it,

00:09:13.052 --> 00:09:14.219
you know, and,

00:09:15.254 --> 00:09:17.607
so I thought he'd you know, seen it.

00:09:17.607 --> 00:09:19.126
And he said, what do I know you from?

00:09:19.126 --> 00:09:21.032
And I said I put myself
on tape for your movie.

00:09:21.032 --> 00:09:22.473
He goes, no, I didn't see any tape.

00:09:22.473 --> 00:09:23.719
He goes what movies have you been in?

00:09:23.719 --> 00:09:25.763
I said I was in a movie
called "Courage Under Fire."

00:09:25.763 --> 00:09:26.596
He goes,

00:09:26.596 --> 00:09:27.429
(snaps fingers)

00:09:27.429 --> 00:09:29.729
You know, I turned to my
wife when I saw that movie,

00:09:29.729 --> 00:09:32.226
and said, "that's the exact
guy I want for Private Ryan,

00:09:32.226 --> 00:09:33.783
"I just wish he wasn't so skinny."

00:09:33.783 --> 00:09:37.089
(audience laughs)

00:09:37.089 --> 00:09:38.598
So.

00:09:38.598 --> 00:09:41.085
- I feel like I read a interview,
at the time, that said,

00:09:41.085 --> 00:09:42.860
like, you couldn't even
kiss your girlfriend,

00:09:42.860 --> 00:09:45.409
cause you could taste
potato chips on her lips?

00:09:45.409 --> 00:09:46.857
- The oil from them, yeah.

00:09:46.857 --> 00:09:48.524
No, I mean, it went,

00:09:49.456 --> 00:09:51.489
people who have those disorders,

00:09:51.489 --> 00:09:52.445
I got it.

00:09:52.445 --> 00:09:54.171
I totally understood it.

00:09:54.171 --> 00:09:56.936
Like if you want to
be, I just told myself,

00:09:56.936 --> 00:09:58.952
I can't allow anything,

00:09:58.952 --> 00:10:01.749
there's no, no compromising.

00:10:01.749 --> 00:10:06.043
None, like, because the
second you open that door,

00:10:06.043 --> 00:10:07.385
you know, it's a slippery slope.

00:10:07.385 --> 00:10:09.541
And I just, I wanted to
look back on it, and go,

00:10:09.541 --> 00:10:11.795
I did absolutely everything I could.

00:10:11.795 --> 00:10:13.838
- [Interviewer] This
relationship between you and Ben.

00:10:13.838 --> 00:10:16.964
It's a kind of, he busts
you, and you bust him back.

00:10:16.964 --> 00:10:19.306
There's a little bit
of that, a lot of that.

00:10:19.306 --> 00:10:20.943
It's the nature of two
guys growing up together.

00:10:20.943 --> 00:10:21.776
- Yeah.

00:10:21.776 --> 00:10:22.609
- [Robin Williams] Yeah.

00:10:22.609 --> 00:10:23.727
- But there's also this thing
that we're talking about,

00:10:23.727 --> 00:10:25.156
we just saw in this scene,
is that there, is that

00:10:25.156 --> 00:10:29.469
who the two of you are in
terms of, to each other?

00:10:29.469 --> 00:10:30.887
- Oh yeah, I mean, we...

00:10:30.887 --> 00:10:31.998
- [Interviewer] Kind of
grown up together in Boston,

00:10:31.998 --> 00:10:33.225
part of Cambridge and all that.

00:10:33.225 --> 00:10:35.580
- Sure, yeah, growing up together,

00:10:35.580 --> 00:10:38.155
best friends all our lives, we don't have,

00:10:38.155 --> 00:10:40.691
I wouldn't say we have moments
that are that, kind of,

00:10:40.691 --> 00:10:43.899
dramatic, where we're like,
listen, man, you know.

00:10:43.899 --> 00:10:47.172
But, you know, I'd lay down
in traffic for the guy.

00:10:47.172 --> 00:10:50.234
I mean, you know, he's my
best pal, you know, I mean.

00:10:50.234 --> 00:10:52.286
You know, I'd pretty
much do anything for him.

00:10:52.286 --> 00:10:54.233
So, yeah, I mean, it's a,

00:10:54.233 --> 00:10:55.970
you know, it's a great
friendship, you know.

00:10:55.970 --> 00:10:58.637
I can't imagine a better way to,

00:11:00.190 --> 00:11:03.191
make a movie, and do something
I love to do that much,

00:11:03.191 --> 00:11:06.199
than with Ben, and to have
this go the way it did,

00:11:06.199 --> 00:11:09.511
and have, this happen, you know.

00:11:09.511 --> 00:11:11.710
And Tom Hanks said something
to me, when we were doing

00:11:11.710 --> 00:11:13.768
"Saving Private Ryan,"
that always stuck with me,

00:11:13.768 --> 00:11:17.109
which was, I was asking
him about, a movie he did

00:11:17.109 --> 00:11:18.388
with Jackie Gleason, and I said,

00:11:18.388 --> 00:11:21.420
what was it like to work
with Jackie Gleason?

00:11:21.420 --> 00:11:23.699
And he thought about it for a
second and he said, you know,

00:11:23.699 --> 00:11:26.699
I made a decision to not be nervous.

00:11:27.557 --> 00:11:28.426
And I went, what?

00:11:28.426 --> 00:11:31.357
And he goes, I knew everyone
was so nervous around him.

00:11:31.357 --> 00:11:33.179
And I just said, you know what,
I'm not going to be nervous

00:11:33.179 --> 00:11:35.187
around him, and he,

00:11:35.187 --> 00:11:37.721
and once he realized I
was treating him like,

00:11:37.721 --> 00:11:39.163
you know, a fellow human being,

00:11:39.163 --> 00:11:42.024
we really had this great
working relationship,

00:11:42.024 --> 00:11:44.379
and it was really good advice, you know.

00:11:44.379 --> 00:11:45.781
- And so you try to do that yourself?

00:11:45.781 --> 00:11:47.594
- I was trying to do it
with Tom Hanks at the time.

00:11:47.594 --> 00:11:48.787
(laughs)

00:11:48.787 --> 00:11:52.120
And, yeah, you don't get anywhere being,

00:11:54.743 --> 00:11:56.940
you know, genuflecting,
to, you know somebody.

00:11:56.940 --> 00:11:59.141
- And I know a lot has been
said and written about the

00:11:59.141 --> 00:12:01.508
experience of "Good Will Hunting," but,

00:12:01.508 --> 00:12:03.249
could you ever have imagined that

00:12:03.249 --> 00:12:04.798
it would have such an effect on people?

00:12:04.798 --> 00:12:07.047
You touched so many lives with that movie,

00:12:07.047 --> 00:12:08.648
it was amazing.

00:12:08.648 --> 00:12:11.638
- It's really great, looking back, I mean,

00:12:11.638 --> 00:12:12.471
it,

00:12:13.349 --> 00:12:16.524
Ben and I had this pact that,

00:12:16.524 --> 00:12:19.230
we just wanted the movie,
that, no matter what,

00:12:19.230 --> 00:12:20.917
we wanted to love it.

00:12:20.917 --> 00:12:23.729
And, even if nobody saw it,

00:12:23.729 --> 00:12:27.363
and it was a great early
lesson, that, you know,

00:12:27.363 --> 00:12:30.074
because even if the movie
hadn't been successful,

00:12:30.074 --> 00:12:31.990
we still would have loved it, and,

00:12:31.990 --> 00:12:34.395
and it would have meant as much to us.

00:12:34.395 --> 00:12:37.887
So I've, you know, not to
be a horrible name dropper,

00:12:37.887 --> 00:12:41.229
but, Clint Eastwood said
to me, a couple years ago,

00:12:41.229 --> 00:12:43.910
I was asking him about, just stuff,

00:12:43.910 --> 00:12:45.476
we were sitting on the set
while they were setting up

00:12:45.476 --> 00:12:47.669
a shot, and, he said, you know,

00:12:47.669 --> 00:12:51.214
people talk about my movies, and they say,

00:12:51.214 --> 00:12:53.616
well, this one's a masterpiece,
or, that one's no good,

00:12:53.616 --> 00:12:55.442
and he goes, I love all of them.

00:12:55.442 --> 00:12:58.199
And I know exactly why
I made each one of them.

00:12:58.199 --> 00:12:59.613
And I wouldn't do anything differently,

00:12:59.613 --> 00:13:00.664
if I had to do it again.

00:13:00.664 --> 00:13:03.392
- [Interviewer] Al Pacino said
the secret to success, is...

00:13:03.392 --> 00:13:04.225
- But what does he know?

00:13:04.225 --> 00:13:05.547
- Yeah, exactly, well.

00:13:05.547 --> 00:13:07.852
I was going to say, he
said the secret to success

00:13:07.852 --> 00:13:09.683
is learning his lines,
or learning your lines.

00:13:09.683 --> 00:13:11.263
What do you reckon the
secret to success is?

00:13:11.263 --> 00:13:13.503
- I never heard that, that's great.

00:13:13.503 --> 00:13:17.310
That is definitely one of
the secrets to success.

00:13:17.310 --> 00:13:21.477
Yeah, having something to say,
and knowing what it is, yeah.

00:13:23.842 --> 00:13:25.251
- Thank you guys so much for watching,

00:13:25.251 --> 00:13:29.094
I made this video because
MrPlaythroughs101 asked me to,

00:13:29.094 --> 00:13:30.987
so if there's a famous
entrepreneur that you want me

00:13:30.987 --> 00:13:33.023
to profile next, leave
it in the comments below,

00:13:33.023 --> 00:13:34.157
and I'll see what I can do.

00:13:34.157 --> 00:13:36.227
I'd also love to know which
of the Top 10 rules here,

00:13:36.227 --> 00:13:37.858
had the biggest impact on you and why,

00:13:37.858 --> 00:13:39.713
what change you're going
to make, what realization

00:13:39.713 --> 00:13:41.527
did you come to after watching this video,

00:13:41.527 --> 00:13:44.626
leave it in the comments, and
I will join in the discussion.

00:13:44.626 --> 00:13:46.684
Finally, want to give a
quick shout out to Xavier,

00:13:46.684 --> 00:13:50.188
from scentlion.com, thank you
so much for buying my book,

00:13:50.188 --> 00:13:51.546
it really means a lot to me.

00:13:51.546 --> 00:13:53.287
Those of you watching, you
want a chance at a shout out

00:13:53.287 --> 00:13:55.026
in a future video, make sure to,

00:13:55.026 --> 00:13:57.309
pick up a copy of the book,
and email in your receipt,

00:13:57.309 --> 00:13:58.254
so we can keep track.

00:13:58.254 --> 00:13:59.957
Thank you guys so much for watching.

00:13:59.957 --> 00:14:02.944
Continue to believe, or
whatever your one word is.

00:14:02.944 --> 00:14:04.741
And I'll see you soon.

00:14:04.741 --> 00:14:08.074
(bullseye sound effect)

00:14:09.963 --> 00:14:13.380
(background crowd noise)

00:14:18.597 --> 00:14:21.319
(girls laugh)

00:14:21.319 --> 00:14:22.152
- [Chuckie] Oh, hello.

00:14:22.152 --> 00:14:22.985
- [Skylar] Oh, hello.

00:14:22.985 --> 00:14:23.944
- [Chuckie] Hi, how are ya?

00:14:23.944 --> 00:14:24.897
- [Skylar] Fine.

00:14:24.897 --> 00:14:26.071
- [Chuckie] So do you ladies...

00:14:26.071 --> 00:14:27.157
- [Skylar] Come here often?

00:14:27.157 --> 00:14:27.990
- [Chuckie] Do I come here?

00:14:27.990 --> 00:14:30.361
I come here a bit, I'm here, eh,

00:14:30.361 --> 00:14:32.172
you know, from time to time.

00:14:32.172 --> 00:14:33.458
Do you go to school here?

00:14:33.458 --> 00:14:34.291
- [Lydia] Yep.

00:14:34.291 --> 00:14:35.745
- Yeah.

00:14:35.745 --> 00:14:38.051
That's it, cause I think
I had a class with you.

00:14:38.051 --> 00:14:40.057
- Oh yeah, what class?

00:14:40.057 --> 00:14:40.890
- History.

00:14:40.890 --> 00:14:41.998
- [Skylar] Maybe.

00:14:41.998 --> 00:14:43.743
- [Chuckie] Yes, I think
that's what it was.

00:14:43.743 --> 00:14:46.948
You don't necessarily, might
not remember me, you know,

00:14:46.948 --> 00:14:48.819
I like it here, it doesn't
mean cause I go here

00:14:48.819 --> 00:14:51.031
I'm a genius, I am very smart.

00:14:51.031 --> 00:14:51.864
- [Clark] Hey.

00:14:51.864 --> 00:14:52.834
- Hey, how's it going, how are ya?

00:14:52.834 --> 00:14:53.801
- [Clark] Good, how you doing?

00:14:53.801 --> 00:14:57.134
What class did you say that was?

00:14:57.134 --> 00:14:58.014
- History.
- History.

00:14:58.014 --> 00:15:00.474
- Yeah, just "History?"

00:15:00.474 --> 00:15:02.273
It must have been a
survey course, then, huh?

00:15:02.273 --> 00:15:03.512
- Yeah, it was, it was surveys.

00:15:03.512 --> 00:15:05.164
You should check it
out, it's a good course.

00:15:05.164 --> 00:15:06.617
It's a pretty good class.

00:15:06.617 --> 00:15:08.116
- [Clark] How'd you like that course?

00:15:08.116 --> 00:15:10.073
- You know, frankly,

00:15:10.073 --> 00:15:12.364
I found the class, you know, rather,

00:15:12.364 --> 00:15:13.205
elementary.

00:15:13.205 --> 00:15:14.038
- Elementary.

00:15:14.038 --> 00:15:15.644
You know, I don't doubt that it was.

00:15:15.644 --> 00:15:16.477
- [Chuckie] Yeah.

00:15:16.477 --> 00:15:19.925
- I eh, I remember that class, it was, um,

00:15:19.925 --> 00:15:22.699
it was just between recess, and lunch.

00:15:22.699 --> 00:15:25.029
- Clark, why don't you go away?

00:15:25.029 --> 00:15:26.320
- [Clark] Why don't you relax?

00:15:26.320 --> 00:15:27.377
- [Skylar] Why don't you just go away?

00:15:27.377 --> 00:15:29.121
- I'm just having fun with
my new friend, that's all.

00:15:29.121 --> 00:15:30.181
- You going to have a problem?

00:15:30.181 --> 00:15:32.491
- No no no no, there's no problem here.

00:15:32.491 --> 00:15:34.944
I was just hoping you
might give me some insight

00:15:34.944 --> 00:15:37.706
into the evolution of the market economy

00:15:37.706 --> 00:15:39.426
in the southern colonies.

00:15:39.426 --> 00:15:43.867
My contention is that, prior
to the Revolutionary War,

00:15:43.867 --> 00:15:46.504
the economic modalities, especially,

00:15:46.504 --> 00:15:50.461
in the southern colonies, could
most aptly be characterized

00:15:50.461 --> 00:15:51.769
as, agrarian...

00:15:51.769 --> 00:15:53.445
- Let me tell you something,
all right, hang on a second.

00:15:53.445 --> 00:15:55.340
- [Will] You're a first-year grad student,

00:15:55.340 --> 00:15:57.467
you just got finished
reading some waxy historian,

00:15:57.467 --> 00:15:58.925
Pete Garrison, probably.

00:15:58.925 --> 00:16:00.374
You're going to be convinced
of that til next month

00:16:00.374 --> 00:16:01.894
when you get to James
Lemon, then you're going to

00:16:01.894 --> 00:16:04.009
be talking about how the
economies of Virginia

00:16:04.009 --> 00:16:06.202
and Pennsylvania were
entrepreneurial and capitalist

00:16:06.202 --> 00:16:07.772
way back in 1740.

00:16:07.772 --> 00:16:09.492
That's going to last until next
year, you're going to be in here

00:16:09.492 --> 00:16:12.199
regurgitating Gordon Wood,
talking about, you know,

00:16:12.199 --> 00:16:15.285
the pre-Revolutionary utopia
and the capital forming

00:16:15.285 --> 00:16:18.773
effects of military mobilization.

00:16:18.773 --> 00:16:21.545
- As a matter of fact, I
won't, because Wood drastically

00:16:21.545 --> 00:16:23.327
underestimates the impact of social...

00:16:23.327 --> 00:16:25.171
- Wood drastically, Wood
drastically underestimates

00:16:25.171 --> 00:16:27.257
the impact of social
distinctions predicated

00:16:27.257 --> 00:16:29.210
upon wealth, especially inherited wealth?

00:16:29.210 --> 00:16:31.677
You got that from Vickers.

00:16:31.677 --> 00:16:34.421
"Work in Essex County," page 98, right?

00:16:34.421 --> 00:16:35.555
Yeah, I read that, too.

00:16:35.555 --> 00:16:36.932
Were you going to plagiarize
the whole thing for us,

00:16:36.932 --> 00:16:41.002
or you have any thoughts
of your own on this matter?

00:16:41.002 --> 00:16:42.675
Or is that your thing,
you just come into a bar,

00:16:42.675 --> 00:16:44.433
you read some obscure
passage, and then pretend

00:16:44.433 --> 00:16:47.184
you pawn it off as your own,

00:16:47.184 --> 00:16:49.362
as your own idea, just
to impress some girls?

00:16:49.362 --> 00:16:51.029
Embarrass my friend?

00:16:51.956 --> 00:16:54.303
See, the sad thing about a
guy like you, is in 50 years,

00:16:54.303 --> 00:16:55.800
you're going to start doing
some thinking on your own,

00:16:55.800 --> 00:16:57.076
and you're going to come up
with the fact that there are

00:16:57.076 --> 00:16:58.745
two certainties in life.

00:16:58.745 --> 00:17:00.973
One, don't do that.

00:17:00.973 --> 00:17:03.902
And two, you dropped 150
grand on a fucking education

00:17:03.902 --> 00:17:06.036
you could have got for a
dollar fifty in lay charges

00:17:06.036 --> 00:17:07.118
at the public library.

00:17:07.118 --> 00:17:09.428
(onlooker laughs)

00:17:09.428 --> 00:17:11.965
- Yeah, but I will have a degree.

00:17:11.965 --> 00:17:14.460
You'll be serving my kids
fries at a drive-through

00:17:14.460 --> 00:17:16.274
on our way to a skiing trip.

00:17:16.274 --> 00:17:17.437
(laughs)

00:17:17.437 --> 00:17:18.779
- Yeah, maybe.

00:17:18.779 --> 00:17:21.493
But at least I won't be unoriginal.

00:17:21.493 --> 00:17:23.238
But I mean, if you have
a problem like that,

00:17:23.238 --> 00:17:26.395
I mean, we could just step
outside, we could figure it out.

00:17:26.395 --> 00:17:29.483
- Nah man, there's no problem.

00:17:29.483 --> 00:17:30.687
It's cool.

00:17:30.687 --> 00:17:31.520
- [Will] It's cool?

00:17:31.520 --> 00:17:32.353
- Yeah.

00:17:32.353 --> 00:17:33.484
- Cool.

00:17:33.484 --> 00:17:34.436
- [Bearded Man] You're
fucking damn right it's cool.

00:17:34.436 --> 00:17:35.507
- [Chuckie] How you like me now?

00:17:35.507 --> 00:17:38.340
(onlooker laughs)

00:17:39.766 --> 00:17:41.494
- [Morgan] My boy's wicked smart.

00:17:41.494 --> 00:17:45.069
